    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, the only test class `AcceptanceTest` passes).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe `namesakeCount` field is a snapshot computed at owner-creation time and returned via `GET /api/owners/{id}`, following the same pattern the codebase already uses for `customerCode` and `householdId`:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a persisted `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter. It must be persisted because the test reads it back through a separate GET request.\n\n2. **DB schemas** \u2014 added `namesake_count` to the `owners` table in all four schemas (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`); tests run on `hsqldb`.\n\n3. **OpenAPI spec** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added `namesakeCount` as a `readOnly` `int32` property on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field and MapStruct auto-maps it (matching name/type \u2014 no mapper change needed).\n\n4. **New function** `AssignNamesakeCount` \u2014 counts existing owners sharing the same `firstName` **and** `lastName`, compared case-insensitively (trim + lowercase). It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the new owner isn't yet in `findAll()` and only *pre-existing* namesakes are counted.\n\n5. **Pipeline wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted the `assignNamesakeCount` step (under `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`.\n\nThe acceptance test creates two owners sharing `Ann <last>` (different postcodes to avoid the household-duplicate 409) and asserts `namesakeCount == 1` on the second \u2014 which now holds, since one prior owner shared the name at creation.",
